# Grants and capabilities

A capability is a named power: the ability to make a network call, read a key, run a
command. A grant is you handing one of those powers to a block. Together they make
the [sandbox](/capabilities/sandboxing-and-the-dock) model concrete — and they live
in the source, so they're reviewable like any other code.

## The capabilities

There is a small, fixed vocabulary of capabilities the [Dock](/capabilities/sandboxing-and-the-dock)
can broker:

- **`net`** — perform an HTTP fetch (host-mediated, SSRF-gated).
- **`kv`** — read and write a tenant-scoped key/value store.
- **`secrets`** — read a named secret value (never the literal, only by key).
- **`fs`** — read and write inside the mounted work area.
- **`exec`** — run a registered command, the seam [toolkits](/tooling/toolkits) use.

Each one maps to a single WIT interface with a single, typed signature. A block that
holds `net` can call `fetch`; it cannot suddenly also write files. Powers don't
bleed.

## Granting a power

A block requests only what it needs. The default is **nothing** — a `sandbox` with no
grants can compute and return a value, and that's all. You add a capability by
naming it, and the weave wires the matching host import into the compiled module:

sandbox python :scrape do
  // needs: net
  import urllib.request
  def run(url):
      return fetch(url)        # the host brokers the call
end

A block that never names `net` has no `fetch` in scope — the import isn't there to
call. The grant *is* the wiring.

## The audit is the point

Because grants are written down and the [one parser](/language/block-grammar) reads
them off the tree, the toolchain can audit them:

work check .        # resolve refs + audit every block's capabilities

`work check` walks the tree and reports what each block requests. A reviewer (or an
agent) sees at a glance that `:scrape` reaches the network and the report-renderer
doesn't. There's no hidden `System.cmd`, no smuggled socket — if a block can touch
the outside world, the grant says so, in the file, in the diff.

## Least power, by default

The model rewards asking for less. Grant `kv` instead of `fs` when you only need a
value by key. Grant nothing when the block is pure. The narrower the grant, the
smaller the audit, and the easier the block is to trust — which is exactly the
property you want when an agent is writing the code.

Next: the powers that are deliberately *not* on this list, and why —
[The three walls](/capabilities/the-three-walls).
